+## Renaming or amending a local entry
+
+The layer is not create-and-delete only — both halves of "change one field
+without re-registering" live in `scripts/lib/profiles/amend.py`, fronted by
+`catalog.sh`:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/catalog.sh rename --slug OLD --to NEW              # move a slug in place
+bash scripts/catalog.sh update --slug SLUG --set KEY=VALUE      # edit registry kwargs (repeatable)
+```
+
+Both write only `registry.local.json` (under `--root`, `--dry-run` supported),
+keep every entry's `engine` field equal to its slug's namespace, and leave the
+curated catalog untouched. `rename` rewrites the `engine` kwarg to follow the
+new slug namespace, so moving to another engine is allowed only when that
+namespace is a **known engine** (core engine profiles + your `engines.d/`
+ones — `register` can create one from evidence). `models.d/` and `composes/`
+are keyed by model id, so a rename never moves files.
+
+`update --set` takes any registry-entry kwarg; values are JSON-shaped
+(`--set drafter=null` clears a drafter, `--set max_ctx=131072` and
+`--set default_port=20231` parse as numbers). `origin` is not editable — it is
+stamped by the loader and changes only when the entry is published to the
+curated catalog. The amended entry is dry-wrapped through the loader before the
+write, so an amend can refuse and leave the layer untouched, but never leave it
+broken.
+
+The shadow case is the reason rename exists: a local slug a later `git pull`
+collides with is kept and *marked* (`shadowed_by_core`), and the listing tells
+you to rename it — which now actually renames it.
